  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened workplace compliance with International Labour Standards in the educational and urban collective transport sectors and for migrants by 2025</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2023, external pressures intensified, marked by persistent conflicts in Eastern Europe and emerging conflicts in the Gaza Strip. These geopolitical tensions compounded inflationary shocks stemming from COVID-19 imbalances and internal crises within retail companies. In Brazil, the new government faced hurdles in advancing its agenda due to opposition parties holding a legislative majority, hindering the promotion of public policies aimed at economic growth and poverty reduction. Brazil&apos;s economic recovery, evident after the peak of the COVID-19 pandemic, saw growth in GDP and employment. In 2023, GDP increased by 2.9%, with the employed population reaching 101 million, the highest since 2012. However, informality and low wages persist, with 39.5 million workers in informal employment and an average real remuneration of R$3,134. Addressing informality and improving labor conditions, including combating child and forced labor, remains crucial for social justice and a fairer future. The ILO Office for Brazil plans strategic collaboration with Brazilian constituents over the next two years to promote labor rights and strengthen tripartite actors and social dialogue as an instrument of democratic governance. Initiatives include strengthening workplace compliance with International Labor Standards in the educational and urban public transport sectors and for migrants by 2025 with the launch of a joint initiative between the Public Ministry of Labor (MPT), Ministry of Labor and Employment (MTE) and Ministry of Education (MEC) in favour of Occupational Health and Safety (OSH) in schools; the strengthening of the capabilities and skills in collective bargaining of the Urban Public Transport Workers Union of Manaus and the launch of the National Migration Policy by the federal government with the establishment of a network of welcoming cities</narrative>
  </description>
